An AirPlay flush drops the partial packet the master session was holding.

airplay_write() collects the player's writes in the master session's
input buffer and sends a packet whenever a full one is there, so between
writes the buffer holds the tail of the last one: up to a packet less a
byte, ten milliseconds of audio from the send position, which runs two
seconds ahead of what the speakers have played. A flush told the device
to drop what it had, but nothing dropped this, so it went out as the head
of the first packet after a resume, spliced onto audio from the resume
point behind it. Two discontinuities in a row, heard as a click at the
start of every resume. Now the flush drops it too, and the timestamp
accounting that counts it (timestamp_set) starts from zero.

--- a/src/outputs/airplay.c
+++ b/src/outputs/airplay.c
@@ -4187,11 +4187,22 @@
 airplay_device_flush(struct output_device *device, int callback_id)
 {
   struct airplay_session *session = device->session;
+  struct airplay_master_session *ams = session->master_session;
 
   if (session->state != AIRPLAY_STATE_STREAMING)
     return 0; // No-op, nothing to flush
 
+  // The tail of the last write that did not fill a packet. The device is
+  // about to drop everything it has, and the player resumes from behind
+  // where this came from, so sending it first would splice ten
+  // milliseconds from ahead of the pause onto the resumed audio: a click.
+  if (ams)
+    {
+      evbuffer_drain(ams->input_buffer, evbuffer_get_length(ams->input_buffer));
+      ams->input_buffer_samples = 0;
+    }
+
   session->callback_id = callback_id;
 
   sequence_start(AIRPLAY_SEQ_FLUSH, session, NULL, "flush");
